# platform — consolidated staging Fly app secrets contract.
#
# This file documents the env vars for the CONSOLIDATED Fly app
# `sindustries-platform-staging`, which carries tasks-api, budget-api,
# content-scheduler-api and auto-post-worker as four processes inside one
# Machine (task 219fb673).
#
# WHY THE NAMES DIFFER FROM THE PER-SERVICE FILES
# -----------------------------------------------
# Fly secrets are app-scoped: every process in one app sees the same
# environment. Three of the four services read `DATABASE_URL`, and the
# staging databases differ by schema (one Neon database, three schemas —
# confirmed by Quinn on task 219fb673). The platform therefore cannot hand
# three different DATABASE_URL values to three children through Fly itself;
# the differentiation happens inside the container.
#
# So the app-level secret is named PLATFORM_<TARGET>_DATABASE_URL, and the
# supervisor (infra/cloud/platform/platform-supervisor.mjs) maps it onto the
# child's own DATABASE_URL. The services read exactly the variable names
# they read today — no service code changes and no service env contract
# changes.
#
# REDIS IS PER SERVICE, NOT SHARED
# --------------------------------
# The approved tech design proposed a single PLATFORM_REDIS_URL on the
# premise that the content-scheduler API and the worker already share one
# Upstash instance. Quinn made that premise conditional on a confirmation
# that has not happened yet, and tasks-api reads REDIS_URL as well. So each
# service keeps its own Redis secret name here. If the instances turn out
# to be identical, collapsing them is a secret rename plus a one-line change
# in infra/cloud/platform/platform-services.mjs — no child, image or deploy
# change.
#
# Owner column maps each key to: Quinn / Rowan / Tom. Quinn-owned values are
# registered into .env.local out-of-band and never committed.
#
# Per-service source contracts (still authoritative for what each service
# reads, and unchanged by the consolidation):
#   infra/cloud/env/tasks-api.env.example
#   infra/cloud/env/budget-api.env.example
#   infra/cloud/env/content-scheduler-api.env.example
#   infra/cloud/env/auto-post-worker.env.example

# ============================================================================
# DATABASE — one per schema (one Neon database, three schemas)
# ============================================================================
# Required in staging.
# Owner: Quinn (Neon staging connection strings).
# Rotation: 90d or on-incident.
# Format: postgresql://<user>:***@<neon-host>/<db>?schema=<schema>&sslmode=require
PLATFORM_TASKS_API_DATABASE_URL=
PLATFORM_BUDGET_API_DATABASE_URL=
# Shared by content-scheduler-api and the auto-post worker — both use the
# content_scheduler schema.
PLATFORM_CONTENT_SCHEDULER_API_DATABASE_URL=

# ============================================================================
# Redis — one per service (see the note above about the shared-instance
# confirmation)
# ============================================================================
# Required in staging for the services that read it.
# Owner: Quinn (Upstash staging REDIS_URL — TLS).
# Rotation: per-deploy.
PLATFORM_TASKS_API_REDIS_URL=
PLATFORM_TASKS_API_CONTENT_SCHEDULER_REDIS_URL=
PLATFORM_CONTENT_SCHEDULER_API_REDIS_URL=
PLATFORM_CONTENT_SCHEDULER_API_CONTENT_SCHEDULER_REDIS_URL=
PLATFORM_AUTO_POST_WORKER_REDIS_URL=
PLATFORM_AUTO_POST_WORKER_CONTENT_SCHEDULER_REDIS_URL=

# ============================================================================
# Cross-service base URL
# ============================================================================
# tasks-api and the auto-post worker both call content-scheduler-api. After
# the consolidation it is reachable on its stable hostname.
# Owner: Quinn. Rotation: per-deploy.
PLATFORM_CONTENT_SCHEDULER_API_BASE_URL=https://content-scheduler-api.staging.sindustries.co.nz/api/v1

# ============================================================================
# CORS allow-lists
# ============================================================================
# NOT secrets — these are plain [env] values in
# infra/cloud/fly/platform.fly.toml, where they live next to the hostname
# matrix they mirror. They are listed here so the full contract is in one
# place; the fly.toml is the source of truth for the values.
PLATFORM_TASKS_API_CORS_ALLOWED_ORIGINS=https://tasks.staging.sindustries.co.nz,https://mission-control.staging.sindustries.co.nz
PLATFORM_BUDGET_API_CORS_ALLOWED_ORIGINS=https://mission-control.staging.sindustries.co.nz
PLATFORM_CONTENT_SCHEDULER_API_CORS_ALLOWED_ORIGINS=https://mission-control.staging.sindustries.co.nz

# ============================================================================
# How these become Fly secrets
# ============================================================================
# The platform app's secrets are registered with:
#
#   fly secrets set --app sindustries-platform-staging \
#     PLATFORM_TASKS_API_DATABASE_URL=... \
#     PLATFORM_BUDGET_API_DATABASE_URL=... \
#     ...
#
# The per-service values keep their existing names — only the database URLs
# change name, because only DATABASE_URL collides across children. Every
# other per-service secret (TASKS_API_*, BUDGET_API_*, AKAHU_*, X_*,
# CONTENT_SCHEDULER_API_*, DEV_SESSION_SECRET, …) is registered UNCHANGED on
# the platform app: the supervisor passes the app environment through to
# each child, so a service reads exactly what it reads today.
#
# The bootstrap script (infra/cloud/scripts/bootstrap-staging.sh) gains a
# fifth service entry for this app; see
# docs/runbooks/platform-consolidation-rollback.md for the cutover order.
